UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54480076459008
DTSTART:20261116T180000Z
DTEND:20261116T190000Z
DESCRIPTION:On 13 October 1775\, the Continental Congress authorized the pu
 rchase of the merchant brigantine Defiance. The ship was acquired in mid-N
 ovember and moored in Wharton and Humphreys shipyard in Philadelphia where
  she was converted into a warship through hull strengthening\, re-rigging\
 , and ordnance and provisioning at a cost of 300-pound sterling. She was n
 amed Andrew Doria after the 16th-century Genoese admiral Andrea Doria. \n\
 nThe Andrew Doria was a 14-gun Brigantine of the Continental Navy. She is 
 most famous for her participation in the Battle of Nassau—the first amph
 ibious engagement by the Continental Navy and the Continental Marines—an
 d for being the first United States vessel to receive a salute from a fore
 ign power.\n\nUnder the command of Captain Isaiah Robinson\, the Andrew Do
 ria sailed down the Delaware River on 17 October 1776\, for a voyage to th
 e West Indies to obtain a cargo of munitions and military supplies at the 
 Dutch island of Sint Eustatius. The Dutch sovereign island of Sint Eustati
 us sold arms and ammunition to anyone willing to pay\, and it was therefor
 e one of the few places from which the young United States could obtain mi
 litary stores. The good relationship between St. Eustatius and the United 
 States resulted in the noted "First Salute".\n\n \n\nOn 16 November 1776\,
  the 14-gun American brig Andrew Doria commanded by Captain Isaiah Robinso
 n sailed\, flying the Continental Colors of the fledgling United States\, 
 into the anchorage below St. Eustatius's Fort Oranje (pronounced “Orange
 ”). Robinson announced his arrival by firing a thirteen-gun salute\, one
  gun for each of the thirteen American colonies in rebellion against Brita
 in. Governor Johannes de Graaff replied with an eleven-gun salute from the
  cannons of Fort Oranje (international protocol required two guns fewer to
  acknowledge a sovereign flag). It was the first international acknowledgm
 ent of American independence. She was carrying a copy of the Declaration o
 f Independence which was presented to Governor De Graaff. The "first salut
 e" was widely reported in the United States at the time\, and later provid
 ed the title for Barbara Tuchman's 1988 book\, The First Salute: A View of
  the American Revolution.\n\nPresident Franklin D. Roosevelt visited St. E
 ustatius for two hours on 27 February 1939 on USS Houston to recognize the
  importance of the 1776 "First Salute". He presented a large brass plaque 
 to St. Eustatius\, displayed today under a flagpole atop the walls of Fort
  Oranje\, reading:\n\n \n\n“In commemoration to the salute to the flag o
 f the United States\, Fired in this fort November 16. 1776\, By order of J
 ohannes de Graaff\, Governor of Saint Eustatius\, In reply to a National G
 un-Salute\, Fired by the United States Brig of War Andrew Doria\, Under Ca
 ptain Isaiah Robinson of the Continental Navy\, Here the sovereignty of th
 e United States of America was first formally acknowledged to a national v
 essel by a foreign official.”\n\nPresented by Franklin Delano Roosevelt\
 , President of the United States of America\n\n \n\nToday\, Citizens of St
 . Eustatius affectionately refer to their island as “Statia” and celeb
 rate Statia Day as a national holiday in celebration of being the first to
  salute the new nation of the United States of America.
GEO:35.103472;-77.035064
LOCATION:Union Point Park\, Outside
SUMMARY:250th Anniversary Celebration of Statia Day
URL;VALUE=URI:https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/250th-anniversary-celebratio
 n-of-statia-day
CATEGORIES:Celebrations & Commemorations
END:VEVENT
