BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar 1 Ics": "https://calendar.unc.edu/event/2026-stone-memorial-lecture-by-dr-alexis-pauline-gumbs"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_1_ics
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261114T061631Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54100076415068
DTSTART:20261117T233000Z
DTEND:20261118T013000Z
DESCRIPTION:Lecture Title: A Homemade Field of Love: Expansive Understandin
 gs of Growth in Times of Change\n\n\nDescription: Community cherished Blac
 k feminist writer and scholar Alexis Pauline Gumbs will lead an interactiv
 e lecture inspired by the “field” as a form of life\, from a field of 
 collard greens\, to the quantum field. These mediations on “the field”
  are inspired by her research on June Jordan\, Fannie Lou Hamer\, George W
 ashington Carver and her most recent book Primary: After and With Alma Tho
 mas. And what fertilizes it all is the legacy of Sonja Haynes Stone whom t
 his lecture celebrates as a founder of the infinitely possible field of Bl
 ack Studies.\n\n \n\nDr. Alexis Pauline Gumbs is a Queer Black Feminist Lo
 ve Evangelist and an aspirational favorite cousin to all life. Her work in
  this lifetime is to facilitate infinite\, unstoppable ancestral love in p
 ractice. Alexis’ books are portable textual ceremonies. Her most recent 
 book Survival is a Promise: The Eternal Life of Audre Lorde revolutionizes
  the scale of life writing. Her award-winning meditation Undrowned: Black 
 Feminist Lessons from Marine Mammals offers the critical interspecies love
  song we need at this urgent stage of our collective evolution. Alexis is 
 also currently working her next book in honor of June Jordan and Fannie Lo
 u Hamer\, A Homemade Field of Love (forthcoming from Yale University Press
 ). Alexis is currently a Monument Lab Fellow and a Loss and Damage Collabo
 rative Commissioned Author creating ceremony in honor of the wisdom of cli
 mate catastrophe survivors in the legacy of Audre Lorde. Alexis received a
  2023 Windham Campbell Prize Winner in Poetry\, 2022 Whiting Award in Nonf
 iction and a 2022 National Endowment for the Arts Fellowship in Prose. Ale
 xis was also a 2020-2021 National Humanities Center Fellow. She has transf
 ormed the scope of intellectual\, creative and oracular writing with her t
 riptych of experimental works published by Duke University Press (Spill: S
 cenes of Black Feminist Fugitivity in 2016\, M Archive: After the End of t
 he World in 2018 and Dub: Finding Ceremony in 2020.) Unlike most academic 
 texts\, Alexis’s work has inspired artists across form to create dance w
 orks\, installation work\, paintings\, processionals\, divination practice
 s\, operas\, quilts and more.\n\n \n\nRead more about the speaker: www.ale
 xispauline.com\n\nRegister now: https://go.unc.edu/StoneMemorialLecture
GEO:35.907852;-79.050272
LOCATION:The Sonja Haynes Stone Center for Research in Black Culture and Hi
 story\, Stone Center Auditorium
SUMMARY:2026 Stone Memorial Lecture by Alexis Pauline Gumbs
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/2026-stone-memorial-lecture-by
 -dr-alexis-pauline-gumbs
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
