BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar 1 Ics": "https://calendar.unc.edu/event/foreign-policy-at-the-ai-frontier-how-ai-is-reshaping-progress-power-and-peace"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_1_ics
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261114T061631Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54471291175921
DTSTART:20261118T223000Z
DTEND:20261118T233000Z
DESCRIPTION:Join the Duke-UNC Rotary Peace Center on Wednesday\, November 1
 8 at the FedEx Global Education Center for the launch of Branka Panic's ne
 w book\, Foreign Policy at the AI Frontier: How AI is Reshaping Progress\,
  Power\, and Peace - a comprehensive guide for anyone navigating AI's tran
 sformative impact on diplomacy and foreign policy.\n\n \n\nOrganized aroun
 d three themes\, the book traces how Progress and Power are being reshaped
  by geopolitical competition\, the rise of Big Tech as quasi-sovereign act
 ors\, and threats from lethal autonomous systems to AI-driven cyber warfar
 e — before turning to Peace\, with real-world applications of AI in dipl
 omacy\, peacebuilding\, and crisis management\, and the Global South's gro
 wing role in shaping AI's future. Blending case studies with ethical refle
 ction\, it closes not with predictions but with aspirations: how nations m
 ight navigate AI rivalry and redirect these technologies toward peace.\n\n
  \n\nThis is not a book about tomorrow\; it's about preparing for tomorrow
  by understanding the choices we make today. Whether you're a diplomat\, a
  technologist\, a student\, or a citizen navigating a changing world\, joi
 n us for a conversation about shaping - not merely inheriting - an AI-tran
 sformed world.\n\n \n\nThis talk will be held in Nelson Mandela Auditorium
  at 5:30pm. Free food will be provided. UNC students can register and rece
 ive CLE credit by signing up at https://heellife.unc.edu/event/12950032. \
 n\n \n\nCan't attend in-person? Join us virtually via Zoom Webinar by regi
 stering here - https://unc.zoom.us/j/94560043198
GEO:35.907828;-79.054177
LOCATION:FedEx Global Education Center\, Nelson Mandela Auditorium (GEC 101
 5)
SUMMARY:Foreign Policy at the AI Frontier: How AI is Reshaping Progress\, P
 ower\, and Peace
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/foreign-policy-at-the-ai-front
 ier-how-ai-is-reshaping-progress-power-and-peace
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar 1 Ics": "https://calendar.unc.edu/event/ballet-bc-medhi-walerski-artistic-director"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_1_ics
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261114T061631Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53682654567272
DTSTART:20261119T000000Z
DTEND:20261119T010000Z
DESCRIPTION:Ballet BC transforms a theater full of strangers into a single\
 , collective audience response—a gasp\, a silence\, a moment of awe.\n\n
  \n\nThis triple bill explores what it means to be human in relationship t
 o one another\, moving from the vulnerable to the virtuosic\, exploring de
 eply personal stories and moments of quiet intimacy to the exhilaration of
  masses of bodies moving together as one. \n\n \n\nThe evening opens with 
 Obsidian by choreographic collaborators Bobbi Jene Smith and Or Schraiber\
 , whose work has been described as “wild and rebellious.” Artistic Dir
 ector Medhi Walerski’s SWAY follows\, inspired by Emily Dickinson’s po
 em “Hope is the thing with feathers” and set to an original score by A
 drien Cronet\, creating moments as fleeting and precarious as the hope des
 cribed in the poem. The evening concludes with Shahar Binyamini’s BOLERO
  X\, set to Ravel’s Boléro\, that builds with pulsating drive and excit
 ement as the ranks of Ballet BC dancers multiply their forces\, turning th
 e stage into a sweeping\, euphoric\, collective—and culminating in a fin
 ale so extraordinary that a room full of strangers can’t help but respon
 d together.\n\n \n\nStudent tickets always $11. Eligible for CLE. Find out
  more and buy tickets at: https://carolinaperformingarts.org/events/ballet
 bc/
GEO:35.911208;-79.051989
LOCATION:Memorial Hall
SUMMARY:Ballet BC Medhi Walerski\, artistic director
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/ballet-bc-medhi-walerski-artis
 tic-director
CATEGORIES:Performances
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events 1 Ics": "https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/mecklenburgs-declaration-ncs-revolutionary-presbyterians"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_1_ics
X-SOURCE:NC Cultural Resources
DTSTAMP:20261114T061558Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53178468622908
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261119
DESCRIPTION:An article published in the April 30\, 1819 edition of the Rale
 igh Register and North Carolina Gazette proclaimed the following: “It is
  not probably known to many of our readers\, that the citizens of Mecklenb
 urg County\, in this State\, made a Declaration of Independence more than 
 a year before Congress made theirs. The following Document on the subject 
 has lately come to the hands of the Editor from unquestionable authority\,
   and is published that it may go down to posterity.”  \n\nThe document 
 in question was called the Mecklenburg Declaration of Independence\, and i
 t had allegedly declared Mecklenburg “free and independent” from Briti
 sh rule on May 20\, 1775. Of the 27 men who were said to have signed the d
 ocument\, almost all were Presbyterian.\n\nBut many people were confused 
 – why were they just now hearing about this revolutionary action? The or
 iginal document burned in a house fire in 1800\, and the published version
  had been reconstructed from notes made by the clerk of the 1775 meeting. 
 No extant copies could be found.\n\nThe debate over the authenticity of th
 e Mecklenburg Declaration began. And\, when a copy of a document called th
 e Mecklenburg Resolves written on May 30\, 1775\, was discovered in a 1775
  newspaper\, the debate intensified. The Resolves were revolutionary\, but
  stopped short of declaring independence. Did both documents exist? Or was
  the Declaration being conflated with the Resolves?\n\nExamine the evidenc
 e and decide for yourself by visiting the new exhibit\, “Mecklenburg Dec
 laration?” at the Presbyterian Heritage Center.\n\nThe PHC is open Tuesd
 ay-Friday (10-4) and Saturday (12-4).
GEO:35.646737;-82.300463
LOCATION:Presbyterian Heritage Center
SUMMARY:Mecklenburg's Declaration: NC's Revolutionary Presbyterians
URL;VALUE=URI:https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/mecklenburgs-declaration-ncs
 -revolutionary-presbyterians
CATEGORIES:Exhibits & Tours
CATEGORIES:Exhibitions
END:VEVENT
