BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-ID:gcal_enoriver_org_9u7o786v1jlk6heq5cp3k1gr1k
SUMMARY:Eno Climate Champions: Building Climate Connections\, Session #1
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261201T163000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261201T180000
DTSTAMP:20261114T061532Z
UID:64s7aaio7s121fm8p9c6jelv59@google.com
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=America/New_York:20261201T163000
SEQUENCE:0
CREATED:20261104T143957Z
DESCRIPTION:<p><b>FREE program for TEENS ages 12–17! Earn volunteer hour
 s\, build leadership skills\, and help your community take action on clima
 te change.</b></p><p><a href="https://www.eventbrite.com/e/eno-climate-cha
 mpions-building-climate-connections-session-1-tickets-2003827745729?aff=od
 dtdtcreator" target="_blank">Register for free here</a>. Limited spots ava
 ilable. </p><p><b>For this program series\, participants will be tasked w
 ith designing an engaging\, interactive climate education table for the E
 no River Association's community events this spring!</b></p><p>As a local 
 land trust dedicated to protecting the Eno River\, ERA recognizes that cli
 mate resilience is an important part of protecting our river and the surro
 unding environment. We believe young people bring valuable perspectives\, 
 creativity\, and ideas to conversations about climate change\, and we want
  to give local teens a meaningful role in shaping how we engage our commun
 ity.</p><p><strong>What will you do?</strong></p><p>Participants will form
  a Teen Advisory Council to help plan\, design\, and facilitate fun\, acce
 ssible educational materials and activities that aim to inspire conversati
 ons with community members about climate change and its connections to the
  Eno River. Throughout the program\, you'll collaborate with other teens\,
  share ideas\, and help bring your vision to life!</p><p>The program will 
 culminate in opportunities to represent the ERA’s Climate Champions at p
 ublic community events\, including Creek Week at the Confluence in March a
 nd the Earth Day Festival for the Eno in April. Participants will help fac
 ilitate the activities they designed and share what they've learned with c
 ommunity members.</p><p><strong>What skills will you build?</strong></p><p
 >Through hands-on activities and collaborative planning\, participants wil
 l have opportunities to develop skills in:</p><ul><li>Environmental educat
 ion and curriculum design</li><li>Science communication and public speakin
 g</li><li>Event planning and activity development</li><li>Environmental ad
 vocacy and community engagement</li><li>Leadership\, teamwork\, and collab
 oration</li><li>Networking and working with a local nonprofit</li></ul><p>
 <strong>Session Details</strong></p><p>The tickets that you reserve on thi
 s Eventbrite page are for the first session of the program series. The sec
 ond session is scheduled for Wednesday\, Jan. 6th\, and the dates of the t
 hird and fourth sessions are TBD. Links to reserve tickets for future sess
 ions will be sent at a later date.</p><p><strong>Participants are </stron
 g><strong>encouraged</strong><strong> to attend as many sessions as possi
 ble</strong>\, ideally all four (but not absolutely mandatory)\, to help c
 arry the project from initial brainstorming through final event preparatio
 n.</p>
LAST-MODIFIED:20261104T144016Z
LOCATION:Orange County Public Library (Main Library)\, 137 W Margaret Ln\,
  Hillsborough\, NC 27278
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
X-SOURCE:Eno River Association
END:VEVENT
