BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Gardens": "https://gardens.duke.edu/event/japanese-tea-gathering-shiwsu-2nd-seating/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:gardens
X-SOURCE:Sarah P. Duke Gardens
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261205T134500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261205T151500
DTSTAMP:20260915T130804Z
CREATED:20260908T202410Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260915T130804Z
UID:17892-1796478300-1796483700@gardens.duke.edu
SUMMARY:Japanese Tea Gathering: Shiwsu -2nd seating
DESCRIPTION:Join tea master Chizuko Sueyoshi and members of the Japanese Tea Practitioners of Durham for a moment of respite in the Duke Gardens teahouse\, where you will experience the centering calm of a Japanese tea gathering in the Urasenke tradition. Enjoy the aesthetics\, poetry and serenity of this rich tradition over an enticing bowl of whisked green tea and a Japanese confection.  Guests will be escorted on a 15-minute walk to the teahouse\, where seating is on the floor unless a chair is requested upon registration by emailing GardensEducation@duke.edu. For ages 12 and up. \nFee: $68; 20% discount for Duke Gardens members with discount code. \nParking: Fees apply \nLocation: Onsite at Duke Gardens. \nAccessibility: This is a small group program that takes place indoors in a non-climate controlled space with floor seating\, in a location that requires outdoors traveling on a combination of paved and unpaved paths with some uneven surfaces and moderate inclines.
URL:https://gardens.duke.edu/event/japanese-tea-gathering-shiwsu-2nd-seating/
LOCATION:Onsite at Duke Gardens – directions to follow
CATEGORIES:Tea Ceremony
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://gardens.duke.edu/wp-content/uploads/AA_Japanese_Teahouse_20250722_KThungtrakul.jpg
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events 1 Ics": "https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/project-restore-educator-trek-waterdog-warriors"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_1_ics
X-SOURCE:NC Cultural Resources
DTSTAMP:20261114T061559Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54496375160756
DTSTART:20261205T140000Z
DTEND:20261205T220000Z
DESCRIPTION:Date: Saturday\, December 5\, 2026. Please also mark your calen
 dar for a rain date on Sunday\, December 6!\nTimes: 9am to 5pm\n\nJoin us 
 in the search for one of North Carolina’s rarest salamanders\, the feder
 ally threatened Neuse River waterdog\, found only in the Neuse and Tar-Pam
 lico River Basins in North Carolina. Learn from expert biologists\, explor
 e the natural history of the waterdog\, observe historically collected spe
 cimens and participate in field monitoring of the species. Discover how yo
 u can bring the excitement of field research of this rare species into you
 r classroom.\n\nWith support from the US Fish and Wildlife Service\, Proje
 ct RESTORE (Rescuing Endangered Species Through Outreach\, Restoration\, a
 nd Education) will feature a suite of educational opportunities for teache
 rs and students to participate in restoration and monitoring of imperiled 
 and endangered species in eastern North Carolina.\n\nLocations/Transportat
 ion: We will meet at the Museum in downtown Raleigh before heading to fiel
 d sites in Johnston County. You can leave your vehicle parked in Raleigh a
 nd ride in Museum vans\; we will return to Raleigh by 5pm.\n\nCredit: 8 co
 ntact hours toward CEUs or Criteria II/III for North Carolina’s Environm
 ental Education Certification Program\n\nRegistration: register now! \n\nI
 f the program you are trying to register for is sold out\, please sign up 
 for the waiting list!\n\nThe full workshop fee is due when you register an
 d payment is via credit card. Registration for most Educator Treks is on a
  first-come\, first-served basis. Treks fill quickly\, so register early!\
 n\nDetails\n\nFee: $25 deposit (will be refunded upon completion of the wo
 rkshop)Requirements: For educators (includes teachers\, administrators\, p
 re-service or substitute teachers\; non-formal educators\; any adult worki
 ng on their EE certification)Program Instructor(s): Megan Davis\, Danielle
  Pender\, Jeff Hall (WRC)Activity Level: Hiking on uneven surfaces and ext
 ended time outdoors in winter weather
GEO:35.782156;-78.639336
LOCATION:North Carolina Museum of Natural Sciences
SUMMARY:Project RESTORE Educator Trek: Waterdog Warriors
URL;VALUE=URI:https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/project-restore-educator-tre
 k-waterdog-warriors
CATEGORIES:Education Programs
CATEGORIES:Workshops & Trainings
CATEGORIES:Outdoor & Recreation
CATEGORIES:Nature & Wildlife Programs
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Gardens": "https://gardens.duke.edu/event/developing-reverent-relationships-with-plants-herbal-stovetop-potpourri/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:gardens
X-SOURCE:Sarah P. Duke Gardens
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261205T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261205T160000
DTSTAMP:20260915T130805Z
CREATED:20260706T123756Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260915T130805Z
UID:17530-1796479200-1796486400@gardens.duke.edu
SUMMARY:Developing Reverent Relationships with Plants: Herbal Stovetop Potpourri
DESCRIPTION:Develop your relationship with some well-known plants by creating a winter-themed stovetop potpourri with Sufia Ikbal-Doucet (they/them)\, herbalist\, ritualist and energy healer. In this hands-on workshop\, we’ll uncover the restorative power of plants like cinnamon\, rosemary\, orange and clove. Learn about their healing properties\, tap into deep presence and intuition to deepen your relationship and create a personalized blend to harmonize your home and spirit. All materials will be provided for each participant to create a stovetop potpourri to take home. \nFee: $42; 20% discount for Duke Gardens. \nParking: Parking fees apply. \nLocation: Pelham-Cullen Classroom inside the Barnes Welcome Center. \nAccessibility: This program takes place seated in an indoor location accessible via paved surfaces and equipped with a hearing loop\, which transmits sound directly to hearing aids and cochlear implants with a T-coil setting. Accessible restrooms are available onsite.
URL:https://gardens.duke.edu/event/developing-reverent-relationships-with-plants-herbal-stovetop-potpourri/
LOCATION:Duke Gardens–Pelham-Cullen Classroom in the Barnes Welcome Center\, 420 Anderson Street\, Durham\, NC\, 27705\, United States
CATEGORIES:Adult Programs
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://gardens.duke.edu/wp-content/uploads/lannon-basil-discovery-garden-7-28-22-scaled.jpg
END:VEVENT
