SUMMARY:Free & Confidential HIV/STI Testing 
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270128T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270128T150000
DTSTAMP:20261114T061542Z
UID:7pe8achei4d9o6djftcff8o2fe_R20260723T170000@google.com__20270128
SEQUENCE:2
CREATED:20260629T193931Z
DESCRIPTION:Durham County’s “I Take Testing Seriously” Program (ITTS
 ) works with community partners and members alike to decrease rates of HIV
 \, Syphilis\, Hepatitis C\, Gonorrhea\, and Chlamydia. Our public health e
 ducators provide comprehensive sexual health services anywhere in Durham C
 ounty.<br><br>When folx attend testing services through the LGBTQ Center o
 f Durham\, they are able to receive: free and confidential HIV/STI screeni
 ngs\, PrEP referrals as needed\, and confidential counseling on next steps
 .<br><br>Specimens are sent to the NC State Lab  for testing where a 4<s
 up>th</sup> generation test is used.<br><br>Access to free barriers (inte
 rnal\, external) and lubrication is always readily available.<br><br>Our t
 ester can also answer questions about PrEP and provide initial steps in th
 e County's Quick Start program for PrEP. Stop in today!<br><br>Are you not
  ready to test? Stop in for an informational session.  All questions welc
 ome.
LAST-MODIFIED:20261029T145049Z
LOCATION:114 Hunt St\, Durham\, NC 27701\, USA
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
X-SOURCE:LGBTQ Center of Durham
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar 1 Ics": "https://calendar.unc.edu/event/introduction-to-qualitative-interviewing"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_1_ics
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261114T061632Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54230780062121
DTSTART:20270128T150000Z
DTEND:20270128T210000Z
DESCRIPTION:While there are ranges of ways to conduct research\, interviewi
 ng is the most common form of data collection in qualitative research\, wi
 th over half of qualitative studies relying on interview-based data. Inter
 views are valuable because they afford researchers opportunities to learn 
 how people understand\, interpret\, and make meaning of their experiences 
 through direct engagement. This introductory course provides practical ski
 lls for both designing and conducting semi-structured qualitative intervie
 ws (along with a brief overview of structured and unstructured interviewin
 g).\n\n \n\nUsing course participants’ research interests\, we will deve
 lop interview protocols\, practice conducting effective interviews—with 
 emphasis on active listening and asking productive follow-up questions. Pa
 rticipants will learn to formulate open-ended questions\, organize intervi
 ew questions around a research question\, and use interview probes to invi
 te elaboration\, clarification\, and reflection. Through examples\, discus
 sion\, and hands-on activities\, we will consider how interviewing is a re
 lational and interpretive process that also requires that researchers refl
 ect on how their own perspectives shape assumptions and decision-making. T
 his course is designed for beginning qualitative researchers and will offe
 r practical opportunities and experiences to plan and conduct semi-structu
 red interviews for qualitative research.\n\n \n\nRegistration and fee (ref
 undable for UNCCH students) is required to secure your spot and for Zoom a
 ccess. Registration covers individual only.
LOCATION:
SUMMARY:Introduction to Qualitative Interviewing
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/introduction-to-qualitative-in
 terviewing
CATEGORIES:Workshops and Classes
END:VEVENT
